WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.895
Here is a problem every modern app has.

00:00:04.895 --> 00:00:07.790
You have a million items.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.684
Each one is a vector, an embedding of hundreds of numbers.

00:00:10.684 --> 00:00:13.579
Someone asks, find the ones most similar to this.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:16.474
Comparing the query against all million is order N.

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:19.369
Too slow at scale.

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:22.263
And a normal hash is no help.

00:00:22.263 --> 00:00:25.158
By design it scatters near-identical inputs to unrelated buckets.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:28.053
So we build a hash that does the opposite.

00:00:28.053 --> 00:00:31.495
This is the whole idea in one picture.

00:00:31.495 --> 00:00:34.936
Every item becomes a vector.

00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:38.378
The vector becomes a short code.

00:00:38.378 --> 00:00:41.820
The code picks a bucket.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.261
The trick is that similar vectors get the same code.

00:00:45.261 --> 00:00:48.703
Here, two cats with almost the same numbers both hash to one zero one one, and land in the same bucket.

00:00:48.703 --> 00:00:52.145
The dog and the car land elsewhere.

00:00:52.145 --> 00:00:55.586
So a search for things like the cat opens one bucket.

00:00:55.586 --> 00:00:59.028
It never compares against the dog or the car at all.

00:00:59.028 --> 00:01:01.824
Where does the code come from?

00:01:01.824 --> 00:01:04.619
Cut the space with a few random lines.

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:07.415
For each line, a point is above it or below it.

00:01:07.415 --> 00:01:10.211
Above is a zero.

00:01:10.211 --> 00:01:13.007
Below is a one.

00:01:13.007 --> 00:01:15.802
Read the lines in order, and you have the code.

00:01:15.802 --> 00:01:18.598
Two points close together sit on the same side of almost every line, so they get the same code.

00:01:18.598 --> 00:01:21.394
Two far-apart points get split by some line, and a bit flips.

00:01:21.394 --> 00:01:24.189
Near vectors collide.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:26.985
Far ones do not.

00:01:26.985 --> 00:01:31.335
That is what locality-sensitive means.

00:01:31.335 --> 00:01:35.685
Closeness survives the hash, instead of being scrambled by it.

00:01:35.685 --> 00:01:40.034
Two points almost on top of each other leave no room for a line to fall between them.

00:01:40.034 --> 00:01:44.384
So nearly every line keeps them together.

00:01:44.384 --> 00:01:48.734
The farther apart they are, the more lines split them, and the lower the chance they share a bucket.

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:53.084
The hash now tracks similarity, which is exactly what an ordinary hash refuses to do.

00:01:53.084 --> 00:01:56.675
How many lines should you use?

00:01:56.675 --> 00:02:00.267
That is the one knob.

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.858
More lines make purer buckets, but they also split more true pairs.

00:02:03.858 --> 00:02:07.449
So you trade recall for precision.

00:02:07.449 --> 00:02:11.041
Pick the number of lines for the recall you need.

00:02:11.041 --> 00:02:14.632
Then run several independent tables, and take the union, to catch the close pairs any single table happens to miss.

00:02:14.632 --> 00:02:18.386
LSH shrinks the search.

00:02:18.386 --> 00:02:22.140
Quantization shrinks the storage.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.894
Instead of keeping each exact vector, you keep a small code for the bucket it falls in.

00:02:25.894 --> 00:02:29.647
A vector that took kilobytes now takes a handful of bytes.

00:02:29.647 --> 00:02:33.401
You keep just enough to remember who is near whom.

00:02:33.401 --> 00:02:37.155
Together, the two ideas make both the search and the storage cheap.

00:02:37.155 --> 00:02:42.542
This is everywhere.

00:02:42.542 --> 00:02:47.929
Facebook, Google, Spotify, and OpenAI all run a version of it, for recommendations and semantic search.

00:02:47.929 --> 00:02:53.316
The same locality trick catches near-duplicate images even after a resize, and groups similar DNA sequences.

00:02:53.316 --> 00:02:58.703
Every one of them gives up the exact answer to win the scale.

00:02:58.703 --> 00:03:04.090
Once you stop demanding exact, find similar and store a billion vectors both become easy.
